---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cta from '../../../components/Cta.astro';
import FaqAccordion from '../../../components/service/FaqAccordion.astro';
import SitemapGenerator from '../../../components/tools/SitemapGenerator.astro';

const path = '/en/tools/sitemap-generator/';

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Is it free? Is there a URL limit?',
    answer:
      "Yes, it's free and you don't need to sign up. I don't cap the number of URLs either: your computer's memory sets the limit. A million URLs takes a few seconds.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Do my URLs go through a server?',
    answer:
      "No. Everything runs in your browser, so neither the list you paste nor the file you upload leaves your computer. You can use it on client sites or projects that aren't live yet.",
  },
  {
    question: 'How should I format the URLs?',
    answer:
      "Absolute and with the protocol: https://example.com/path, not /path. For the homepage, keep the trailing slash (https://example.com/). Anything that doesn't pass shows up as skipped so you can review it line by line.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I upload a Screaming Frog CSV?',
    answer:
      'Yes. If the file is a .csv, the generator looks for the URL column and ignores the rest (status codes, titles and so on). With a .txt it reads one URL per line.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I have more than 50,000 URLs?',
    answer:
      'Each sitemap holds up to 50,000 URLs or 50 MB. Go over and the generator splits them into sitemap-1.xml, sitemap-2.xml… plus a sitemap-index.xml linking them all. One million URLs gives you 20 sitemaps and an index.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What if my URLs come from several domains?',
    answer:
      'The generator groups by domain out of the box. You download a ZIP with one folder per domain, each with its own sitemap or index, ready to upload to its site.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I put several subdomains in the same sitemap?',
    answer:
      'In the advanced settings, change the RegExp from .* to example\\..* (with your own domain, obviously). That way www, m and blog on example.com end up in the same sitemap.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I tick the lastmod option?',
    answer:
      "Only if every URL changed today. Stamp today's date on pages that haven't changed in months and Google ends up ignoring lastmod on your site.",
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I submit the sitemap to Google?',
    answer:
      'Upload it to your domain root, add the line Sitemap: https://yourdomain.com/sitemap.xml to your robots.txt and submit it in Search Console under Sitemaps. Then repeat in Bing Webmaster Tools.',
  },
];

const xmlSample = `<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
  <url>
    <loc>https://example.com/</loc>
  </url>
  <url>
    <loc>https://example.com/blog/</loc>
  </url>
</urlset>`;

const bulkSample = `sitemap-index.xml
sitemap-1.xml
sitemap-2.xml
...
sitemap-20.xml`;

const domainsSample = `example.com/
  sitemap-index.xml
  sitemap-1.xml
  sitemap-2.xml
example.es/
  sitemap.xml`;

const preClass = 'rounded-lg bg-dark font-mono text-on-dark';

const webApplication = {
  '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'XML Sitemap Generator',
  url: `https://nachomascort.com${path}`,
  applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Any',
  browserRequirements: 'Requires JavaScript',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  author: { '@id': 'https://nachomascort.com/#person' },
};
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  title="Free XML Sitemap Generator Online — Nacho Mascort"
  description="Build a sitemap.xml for free from a list of URLs or a CSV. Splits into sitemap indexes past 50,000 URLs, groups by domain and never sends your data to a server."
  path={path}
  alternatePath="/herramientas/generador-sitemap/"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/en/' },
    { name: 'Tools', path: '/en/tools/' },
    { name: 'XML Sitemap Generator', path },
  ]}
  faq={faq}
  extraSchemaNodes={[webApplication]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pt-16 sm:px-6 sm:pt-20">
    <p class="meta"><a href="/en/tools/" class="hover:text-ink">Tools</a> · Technical SEO</p>
    <h1 class="display-1 mt-5">XML Sitemap Generator</h1>
    <p class="lede mt-6">
      Paste your URLs or upload a file and get a sitemap.xml ready for Search Console in one click. It's free, there's no
      sign-up and it handles millions of URLs. Everything happens in your browser, so your URLs never leave your computer.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm text-muted">
      <li><span class="text-accent-ink" aria-hidden="true">✓</span> Automatic indexes past 50,000 URLs</li>
      <li><span class="text-accent-ink" aria-hidden="true">✓</span> One sitemap per domain</li>
      <li><span class="text-accent-ink" aria-hidden="true">✓</span> Your URLs stay on your device</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <SitemapGenerator lang="en" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pt-16 sm:px-6 md:pt-24">
    <p class="section-label">Guide</p>
    <h2 class="display-2 mt-3 max-w-[24ch]">A quick guide to sitemaps</h2>

    <div class="article-body mt-4">
      <h2>What is an XML sitemap?</h2>
      <p>Let's start from the beginning.</p>
      <p>
        An XML sitemap is a file listing the URLs on your site that you want Googlebot and friends to crawl. This is what
        one looks like raw:
      </p>
      <pre class={preClass}><code>{xmlSample}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <strong>A sitemap doesn't replace internal linking.</strong> It gives Google a shortcut to discover new pages and
        spot the ones that changed since its last visit.
      </p>

      <h3>Do I need one?</h3>
      <p>It depends on the size of your site.</p>
      <p>
        If you have 20 well-linked pages, Google will find them anyway. I'd still have one, because Search Console uses it
        to tell you how many of those URLs are indexed.
      </p>
      <p>Where it does make a difference:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Sites with thousands of pages or that grow every day: ecommerce, publishers, directories, programmatic SEO…</li>
        <li>Freshly published pages or pages with few internal links.</li>
        <li>Migrations, when you want Google to find the new URLs as soon as possible.</li>
        <li>CMSs that don't generate a sitemap, or fill it with URLs you don't want indexed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How does the generator work?</h2>
      <p>
        This tool used to live at sitemapgenerator.xyz. I moved it to my site and rebuilt it so everything happens in your
        browser, which means your URLs don't go through any server.
      </p>
      <p>Three steps:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Get your URLs.</strong> The quickest way is to run Screaming Frog, filter by indexable and export. A CMS
          export or a database query works too.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Paste them or upload the file.</strong> If there aren't many (up to about 10,000), paste them in the box.
          If there are more, upload a .txt with one URL per line or the crawler's .csv as is: the generator finds the URL
          column and ignores the rest.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hit generate and download.</strong> One sitemap means you get the .xml. Several, and you get them in a
          ZIP.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Along the way it removes duplicates, escapes special characters and shows you the lines it skipped. The escaping
        part matters: <strong>a single unescaped <code>&amp;</code> breaks the whole sitemap</strong> and Search Console
        flags it as an error.
      </p>

      <h2>What if I have more than 50,000 URLs?</h2>
      <p>
        Each sitemap takes up to 50,000 URLs and 50 MB uncompressed. Go over either limit and the generator splits the list
        and creates an index pointing to every chunk.
      </p>
      <p>Say you have a million URLs. You'll get this:</p>
      <pre class={preClass}><code>{bulkSample}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The index points to each sitemap at your domain root (<code>https://yourdomain.com/sitemap-1.xml</code>), so upload
        every file there. Then submit only the index to Search Console and Google takes care of the rest.
      </p>

      <h2>What if I work with several domains?</h2>
      <p>
        A sitemap can only hold URLs from its own host, so the generator groups them by domain without you touching
        anything.
      </p>
      <p>Mix URLs from example.com and example.es and the ZIP looks like this:</p>
      <pre class={preClass}><code>{domainsSample}</code></pre>

      <h3>What if I want to merge subdomains?</h3>
      <p>
        You can. Open the advanced settings and change the grouping RegExp, which defaults to <code>.*</code> (one group per
        host).
      </p>
      <p>
        If you have www.example.com, m.example.com and blog.example.com and want them in the same sitemap, swap
        <code>.*</code> for <code>example\..*</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Heads up: <strong>Google only accepts URLs from other hosts in a sitemap if you've verified all of them in Search
        Console</strong> or reference the sitemap from each host's robots.txt.
      </p>

      <h2>How do I submit it to Google?</h2>
      <p>Once you've downloaded it, four steps left:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Upload the file to your root: <code>https://yourdomain.com/sitemap.xml</code>.</li>
        <li>Add this line to your robots.txt: <code>Sitemap: https://yourdomain.com/sitemap.xml</code></li>
        <li>In Search Console, go to Indexing › Sitemaps, paste the URL and hit Submit.</li>
        <li>Do the same in Bing Webmaster Tools, which also feeds Copilot and DuckDuckGo.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A few days later the Sitemaps report tells you how many URLs Google discovered and how many it indexed.
        <strong>If the gap is wide, you know where your next audit starts.</strong>
      </p>

      <h2>Which URLs go in the sitemap?</h2>
      <p>Only the ones you want to see in Google. That means URLs that:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Return a 200, no redirects.</li>
        <li>Are canonical, without tracking parameters or duplicate versions.</li>
        <li>Have no <code>noindex</code> and aren't blocked by robots.txt.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every broken or redirected URL you add tells Google your sitemap isn't reliable, and over time it pays less
        attention to it. That's why I'd run the crawler first and keep only the indexable pages.
      </p>

      <h2>What about lastmod, priority and changefreq?</h2>
      <p>Google ignores <code>priority</code> and <code>changefreq</code>, so the generator doesn't even add them.</p>
      <p>
        <code>lastmod</code> is a different story. Google does use it, but only when the date reflects a real content
        change. Stamp today's date on pages that haven't changed in months and it learns to ignore it on your site.
      </p>
      <p>
        My advice: tick the option only if every URL in your list changed today. Otherwise, let your CMS set the dates page
        by page.
      </p>
      <p>And that's it. If you found it useful, share it with whoever is fighting with sitemaps right now 🙂</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <FaqAccordion lang="en" faq={faq} />

  <Cta lang="en" title="Is your site struggling with indexing?" />
</Base>
